# The probability of the flip is the variable p, never assigned, so the bound is asked for
# every initial p.  Where p >= 2 the flip sets c to 1 or leaves it 0, so whatever distribution
# the flip is given, E[c] <= 1 there; elsewhere c = 2.  The lower bound 1.5 is false at every
# p >= 2.  A probability outside [0, 1] makes the run abort (it contributes 0), so the
# preexpectation is 2 for p < 2 and 0 for p >= 2.
# Expected: unknown.  The choice rule applied at p >= 2 gives p * 1 + (1 - p) * 0 = p >= 1.5,
# and the false bound was proved (valid).
c := 0;
if p >= 2 {
	if flip(p) {
		c := 1
	}
} else {
	c := 2
}
return c
?Ex[c] >= 1.5
